+This chapter connects the actuator force characterization of Chapter~\ref{ch:muscles} to torque about the robot knee. The rigid-body torque model of the earlier robot work assumed inextensible muscle paths on rigid links, and the isometric tests reported here show where that assumption fails and by how much. The chapter proceeds from the simple pinned knee to the biomimetic joint, identifies the correction terms that remove the rigid-body idealization from the torque prediction, validates them on held-out tests, and closes with the attachment-route redesign that brings the actuator configurations to human-scaled torque targets. The methods, results, and discussion follow the manuscript noted above; the physical-validation paragraph of Section~\ref{sec:disc_pulley} additionally reports the newer nine-point sweep results, which postdate the manuscript draft.
